; FACTORIAL -- the witness precision-by-declaration.md named for why the
; representation solver has to be BIDIRECTIONAL.
;
;   "(fact (int 0 30)) has every input small and an accumulator reaching
;    30! ~ 2.65x10^32, so the pressure comes from the declared RESULT and
;    nothing forward makes `acc` big. A forward-only solver would pass the
;    entire current corpus and fail on the first factorial."
;
; That is this file. `n` is at most 30 and `i` at most 31; both are machine
; words and stay machine words. Only `acc` is a bignum, and the only thing that
; says so is the declared result -- read backwards through the loop's exit.
;
; AND `i` STAYING A WORD IS THE HALF THAT IS EASY TO GET WRONG. It is read by
; the big multiply, so a solver that promoted everything a big operation touches
; would give this loop a bignum counter and a bignum comparison. The rule is
; gated on provability: a name a big operation reads is promoted only if it is
; NOT provably inside the window, and `i` is provably 1..31. It is widened at
; the multiply instead, which is one conversion per iteration against a bignum
; increment and a bignum compare.
;
; 30! = 265252859812191058636308480000000, which is under 2^110.

(export fact)

(sig fact ((n (int 0 30))) (int 0 +inf))

; `i` starts at 2 rather than 1 because multiplying by 1 is a wasted bignum
; operation and an allocation -- fact(0) and fact(1) are both 1 and the guard
; already gives them.
(def fact (fn (n)
  (loop ((acc 1) (i 2))
    (> i n)  acc
    else     (again (* acc i) (+ i 1)))))
